{:edb/db-before-t 1782 :edb/db-after-t 1783 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535095 50 #inst "2026-10-08T16:43:46.341Z" 13194139535095 true] [17592186124356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330967 13194139535095 true] [17592186124356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330974 13194139535095 true] [17592186124357 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330981 13194139535095 true] [17592186124357 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330988 13194139535095 true] [17592186124874 1017 #edb/ref 17592186122266 13194139535095 false] [17592186124874 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330995 13194139535095 true] [17592186124874 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535095 true] [17592186124874 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z = f(x, y)$, with the plane $x = 1$. Given that the point $P(1, 2, 4)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{y} = \\frac{3x + y}{\\sqrt{6xy + y^{2}}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." 13194139535095 false] [17592186124874 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{6xy+{y}^{2}}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139535095 true] [17592186124874 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =\\frac{3(1)+(2)}{\\sqrt{6(1)(2)+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{5}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330967 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330968 13194139535095 true] [17592186330967 1089 "q-132587" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330967 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535095 true] [17592186330967 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2xy+2$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330967 1113 #uuid "65b6a4c9-d497-51fc-b78a-818d9d90b8fc" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330967 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)(2)+2 \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =6(x-1) \\\\ z & =6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330969 13194139535095 true] [17592186330968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330970 13194139535095 true] [17592186330968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330971 13194139535095 true] [17592186330968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330972 13194139535095 true] [17592186330968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330973 13194139535095 true] [17592186330968 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535095 true] [17592186330968 1041 "selection" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330968 1104 #edb/ref 17592186330972 13194139535095 true] [17592186330968 1110 #uuid "ae4e4c26-14dd-5edd-9ebd-badb52824707" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330969 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-5\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330969 1111 #uuid "5535b1d3-0529-5411-b513-6a8eeebc8b8e" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330970 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-2\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330970 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330970 1111 #uuid "2e60d34e-02b2-5978-b117-a667cb9ca579" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330971 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x+3\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330971 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330971 1111 #uuid "51b22bb7-db06-5bad-9384-34af6ed679e8" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330972 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330972 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330972 1111 #uuid "62be1fc5-e9da-52c3-914b-0cf766b7145d" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330973 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+3\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330973 1111 #uuid "c75587ad-2a40-5b14-91f0-e672131fad70" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330974 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330975 13194139535095 true] [17592186330974 1089 "q-132595" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330974 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535095 true] [17592186330974 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330974 1113 #uuid "90feb6cc-3983-5462-9881-e7dbb7d7a0f5" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330974 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330976 13194139535095 true] [17592186330975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330977 13194139535095 true] [17592186330975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330978 13194139535095 true] [17592186330975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330979 13194139535095 true] [17592186330975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330980 13194139535095 true] [17592186330975 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535095 true] [17592186330975 1041 "selection" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330975 1104 #edb/ref 17592186330976 13194139535095 true] [17592186330975 1110 #uuid "42ac90ee-1bdb-505d-b1b9-a5d78e62d51e" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330976 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330976 1111 #uuid "f43633ae-9118-5fb5-8924-8d9d38ed6b6c" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330977 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{2}y+1\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330977 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330977 1111 #uuid "dd66e5b1-b0d7-510d-902f-6af28619ad8c" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330978 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2}y-1\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330978 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330978 1111 #uuid "9a3342aa-db4c-58cc-810a-00e95c556260" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330979 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330979 1111 #uuid "9ad33dab-823c-5cbf-aae5-94ac4bbd9ed6" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330980 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{4}y-2\\end{aligned}" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330980 1111 #uuid "f5e4bec9-e9a6-5d37-acc4-6b6af9581f4a" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330981 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330982 13194139535095 true] [17592186330981 1089 "q-132601" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330981 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535095 true] [17592186330981 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330981 1113 #uuid "3e93bbd2-d64e-547a-8b0e-91b9a4e6aa38" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330981 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j+2\\,k.$" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330983 13194139535095 true] [17592186330982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330984 13194139535095 true] [17592186330982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330985 13194139535095 true] [17592186330982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330986 13194139535095 true] [17592186330982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330987 13194139535095 true] [17592186330982 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535095 true] [17592186330982 1041 "selection" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330982 1104 #edb/ref 17592186330984 13194139535095 true] [17592186330982 1110 #uuid "38009d16-d2ad-5654-ae55-e75bed727df8" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330983 1034 "j-3\\,k" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330983 1111 #uuid "cfd92310-90b2-582f-84db-234cd0ca623c" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330984 1034 "j+2\\,k" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330984 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330984 1111 #uuid "ca3ce60a-93d2-53e6-8882-c89d623a9e10" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330985 1034 "-2\\,j+k" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330985 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330985 1111 #uuid "770e6717-4d6d-56b4-b15d-1143ab390c4f" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330986 1034 "3\\,j+k" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330986 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330986 1111 #uuid "fcd669da-ce2e-57b5-af70-442799426116" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330987 1034 "-2\\,j+3\\,k" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330987 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535095 true] [17592186330987 1111 #uuid "1e2ced03-0d0e-5009-83cc-26a21ff53991" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330988 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330989 13194139535095 true] [17592186330988 1089 "q-132602" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330988 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535095 true] [17592186330988 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,7)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330988 1113 #uuid "e7a0ef21-1ab3-5b2b-a1e5-8efa25c0c00b" 13194139535095 true] [17592186330988 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\time